WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.360
Automation can make DFIR work faster and more consistent, but only when it is prepared and tested before the incident.

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:15.780
Repeatable collection scripts can gather approved logs, system information, process lists, configuration data, and selected artifacts in a consistent format.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:22.480
Parsing utilities can normalize evidence, extract fields, or convert exports into a timeline-friendly structure.

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:25.180
The benefit is repeatability, not cleverness.

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:29.160
Triage checklists and environment setup scripts are also useful.

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:36.660
A checklist can guide responders through early questions, required approvals, preservation steps, and initial scope decisions.

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:43.520
Setup scripts can prepare analysis workstations with expected folders, dependencies, and verified tool versions.

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:49.420
These small efficiencies matter when the team is tired, the incident is moving, and mistakes are easy to make.

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:51.160
Validation is essential.

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:56.020
Scripts should be tested with sample data, known-good cases, and realistic edge cases.

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:02.400
They should fail safely, preserve original evidence, log their actions, and make outputs easy to review.

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:07.500
A script that silently skips errors or overwrites evidence is dangerous.

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:13.720
A script that records what it did, where outputs went, and what errors occurred is much easier to trust.

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:17.140
Avoid destructive or untested automation during incidents.

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:21.600
Do not run a new cleanup script against production systems just because it seems convenient.

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:26.220
Do not collect broadly without considering privacy, business impact, or authority.

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:30.280
Automation should have safe defaults, clear scope, and human review.

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:35.700
In DFIR, speed helps only when the output remains reliable and the response remains controlled.
